      "raw": "{\"ruleId\":\"item-0604429e5dd4\",\"class\":\"partial\",\"constraints\":[{\"id\":\"md-image-empty-alt\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"pattern\":\"!\\\\[\\\\s*\\\\][(\\\\[]\",\"path\":\".floor/files/**\"},{\"id\":\"html-img-empty-alt\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"pattern\":\"<[iI][mM][gG]\\\\b[^>]*\\\\b[aA][lL][tT]\\\\s*=\\\\s*(\\\"\\\\s*\\\"|'\\\\s*')\",\"path\":\".floor/files/**\"},{\"id\":\"html-img-missing-alt\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"pattern\":\"<[iI][mM][gG]\\\\b(?![^>]*\\\\b[aA][lL][tT]\\\\s*=)[^>]*>\",\"path\":\".floor/files/**\"}],\"coverage\":\"Flags images that plainly have no alt text: Markdown images with an empty alt (inline or reference style), HTML <img> tags with an empty or blank alt attribute, and single-line HTML <img> tags with no alt attribute at all.\",\"residual\":\"Does every image in the document have alt text that actually says what the image shows, rather than missing, empty, placeholder or non-descriptive alt text? This includes image syntaxes or tags not caught by the patterns and img tags that span several lines.\",\"probes\":{\"violating\":[{\"path\":\"docs/readme.md\",\"content\":\"# Pets\\n\\n![](cat.png)\\n\"},{\"path\":\"docs/page.html\",\"content\":\"<p>Sales</p>\\n<img src=\\\"chart.png\\\" alt=\\\"\\\">\\n\"},{\"path\":\"docs/other.html\",\"content\":\"<p>Logo</p>\\n<img src=\\\"logo.png\\\">\\n\"}],\"compliant\":{\"path\":\"docs/readme.md\",\"content\":\"# Pets\\n\\n![A grey cat sleeping on a sofa](cat.png)\\n\\n<img src=\\\"chart.png\\\" alt=\\\"Bar chart of quarterly sales rising from 10 to 40\\\">\\n\"}},\"rationale\":\"Missing or empty alt text is mechanically detectable for Markdown and single-line HTML images. Whether existing alt text actually describes what the image shows is a judgement about meaning. That covers placeholders like 'image' or a filename. Multi-line tags and other image syntaxes also escape line-based patterns. So the rule is only partially decidable.\"}",
